  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe synoptic environment continues to evolve as expected given the \nhighly amplified omega block draped across CONUS. Southeast Michigan \nremains positioned near the eastern longwave trough axis which will \nundergo a restructuring during the course of the day. The primary \nupper low is expected to become absorbed by a retrograding 528 dam \n500 mb low tracking southwestward along the St. Lawrence River, \nwhich then settles over the Northeast this evening. Meanwhile, \nupstream longwave ridging drifts eastward, easing into the Upper \nMidwest/Minnesota. This results in the local area becoming \npositioned between the complementary waves while an embedded speed \nmax transits due south over the central Great Lakes. The CVA induces \nsubtle forcing aloft to maintain localized areas of lake effect \nprecipitation for most of the day. However, coverage will be limited \nto the eastern Thumb and locations along the immediate southern Lake \nHuron shoreline, after northerly flow backs north-northwesterly \nlater today.\n\nThermodynamically, deterministic solutions indicate slight \ndegradation of the existing thermal trough as weak warm advection \nslips in from the encroaching ridge. 850 mb temperatures should lift \nby 2 or 3 C today, but still anomalously cold, resulting in highs \nonly in the mid 40s. Regarding precipitation type within the narrow \ncorridor of lake effect showers, forecast soundings reveal eventual \ndrying through the DGZ as flow backs between 5 and 7 kft AGL. This \nlimits the likelihood for ice nuclei, therefore, rain is the \npreferred hydrometeor form as the day progresses. Note that some \nsnowflakes could still mix in during the morning hours today given \ndeeper saturation depths. Not as chilly overnight thanks to \ninsulating lake stratocumulus, but still colder than normal.\n\nThe lower tropospheric anticyclone builds northeastward out of the \nMid-Mississippi Valley Sunday, weakening boundary layer flow. Lower \ncolumn winds now look to retain a northwesterly, if not north-\nnorthwesterly trajectory well into the afternoon hours, but the lack \nof shear should suffice in concluding residual lake effect \nprecipitation by mid afternoon. Likewise, lake induced clouds will \nlinger into Sunday evening, and likely into the overnight hours. \nSignificant column drying and subsidence ensue Sunday night as the \nlongwave ridge crosses overhead. 1030 mb surface high pressure \ngradually builds into the Ohio Valley with time which flips weak \ngradient flow southwesterly.\n\nTemperatures moderate throughout next week as the ridge flattens but \nlingers from southern Appalachia into the Southeast. This preserves \nan increasingly confluent stream of milder Gulf-modified air from \nthe southwest. A progressive Pacific wave unblocks the pattern and \nis what causes the ridge to de-amplify, but mid-range guidance \ncontinues to delay the arrival of embedded shorter wavelength \ncyclonic features until Thursday or Friday. Still, most of the QPF \nassociated with the passing surface reflection tracking across the \nUP/Lake Superior also holds farther north. Solutions also differ in \nthe passage of the attendant cold front, with warm advection \npossibly hanging on as the next wave's deeper core preserves \nsouthwesterly flow into the weekend. Global models suggests 850 mb \ntemperatures could make a run at 10C late next week, suggesting 2m \ntemperatures above 60F may be achievable. Some ensembles families \nsupport this case, in addition to the lack of measurable \nprecipitation before the weekend.",
